{
  "segment": "S3",
  "entry": "shock_case",
  "nodes": {
    "shock_case": {
      "role": "probe",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "A 40-year-old has a 35% scald to the chest and both legs, seen 6 hours after the burn.",
          "claims": [
            "K18"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "BP 85/50, pulse 130, urine 15 mL/h. Haematocrit has risen from 42 to 55. Temperature 37.2 °C. What type of shock is this?",
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Septic shock, from the infected burn",
          "next": "shock_septic",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR31"
        },
        {
          "label": "Hypovolaemic shock, from plasma loss",
          "next": "shock_ok",
          "kind": "correct"
        },
        {
          "label": "Cardiogenic shock, from a failing pump",
          "next": "shock_cardio",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR31"
        },
        {
          "label": "Not sure, give me a hint",
          "next": "shock_hint",
          "kind": "help"
        }
      ],
      "ask_confidence": true
    },
    "shock_hint": {
      "role": "hint",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Ask what has left the circulation. Haematocrit rises when plasma leaves the vessels, and urine falls with it.",
          "claims": [
            "K18"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "shock_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_septic": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. Infection is a later threat, not the first cause of shock in the opening hours.",
          "claims": [
            "K66"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Look at the haematocrit. Which way is it moving, and what does that say is leaving the blood?",
          "claims": [
            "K18"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Show me why",
          "next": "shock_septic_why",
          "kind": "deeper"
        },
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "shock_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_septic_why": {
      "role": "explain",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Septic shock tempts because burns do get infected, and sepsis is a real killer after the first 48 to 72 hours.",
          "claims": [
            "K66"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "But early burn shock is predominantly hypovolaemic: capillary leak moves plasma out, haematocrit rises and urine falls. Heart depression is an add-on.",
          "claims": [
            "K19",
            "K18"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "shock_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_cardio": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. A weak pump is only a secondary factor in early burn shock.",
          "claims": [
            "K19"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Check the blood count and the urine. What do they say about the volume that is missing?",
          "claims": [
            "K31"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Show me why",
          "next": "shock_cardio_why",
          "kind": "deeper"
        },
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "shock_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_cardio_why": {
      "role": "explain",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Cardiogenic shock tempts because the burn does depress myocardial function, and that part is real.",
          "claims": [
            "K19"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "But the main problem is plasma loss: haematocrit rises and urine falls, so volume comes first.",
          "claims": [
            "K19",
            "K31"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "shock_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_ok": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Yes. A rising haematocrit and low urine output both mean plasma is leaving the circulation.",
          "claims": [
            "K18",
            "K31"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Burn shock is predominantly hypovolaemic, with some myocardial depression from mediators.",
          "claims": [
            "K19"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Why does plasma leak so fast?",
          "next": "shock_why",
          "kind": "deeper"
        },
        {
          "label": "Next",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "continue"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shock_why": {
      "role": "explain",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "A local burn releases histamine, prostaglandins and free radicals, which make the capillaries leaky.",
          "claims": [
            "K17"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The leak starts within minutes, peaks at 8 to 12 hours and runs on for up to 24 hours.",
          "claims": [
            "K17"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Next",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "continue"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_probe": {
      "role": "probe",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Still in the first 24 hours. The heart is a little depressed. What is the first-line treatment for this shock?",
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Inotropes to support the failing heart",
          "next": "fluid_t31",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR31"
        },
        {
          "label": "Albumin infusion at full dose from the start",
          "next": "fluid_t8",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR8"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ringer lactate titrated to urine output",
          "next": "fluid_ok",
          "kind": "correct"
        },
        {
          "label": "Not sure, give me a hint",
          "next": "fluid_hint",
          "kind": "help"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_hint": {
      "role": "hint",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Ask what the body is short of. The missing part is plasma volume, and it is the volume that goes back first.",
          "claims": [
            "K19"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_t31": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. Inotropes treat a weak heart, and the heart is not the main problem in burn shock.",
          "claims": [
            "K19"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "What is missing from the circulation? Think about the haematocrit and the urine again.",
          "claims": [
            "K18",
            "K31"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_t8": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. Albumin has a role in burn care, but it is not the first fluid at the start.",
          "claims": [
            "K46"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Check the timing. What does classic practice give in the first hours, and when does colloid come in?",
          "claims": [
            "K46"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Show me why",
          "next": "fluid_t8_why",
          "kind": "deeper"
        },
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_t8_why": {
      "role": "explain",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Albumin tempts because burns do lose protein, and albumin is a familiar fluid on the ward.",
          "claims": [
            "K18"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "But the leak is the first problem, and classic Parkland gives crystalloid alone in the first 24 hours. Colloid comes later, about 8 to 24 hours, or when needs are very high.",
          "claims": [
            "K46"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "fluid_probe",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fluid_ok": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O4"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Yes. Ringer lactate is the fluid of choice, and it is titrated to urine output rather than fixed.",
          "claims": [
            "K68",
            "K1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Large volumes of saline cause hyperchloraemic acidosis, which is why Ringer lactate is preferred.",
          "claims": [
            "K54"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In classic Parkland the first 24 hours are crystalloid only. Evans and Brooke carry colloid, with dextrose in water.",
          "claims": [
            "K46",
            "K87",
            "K50"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Next",
          "next": "depth_case",
          "kind": "continue"
        }
      ]
    },
    "depth_case": {
      "role": "probe",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "A forearm burn, mottled red and white. It hurts less than the skin around it, and it refills slowly when pressed.",
          "claims": [
            "K25"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "What depth is it?",
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Full thickness burn",
          "next": "depth_t_full",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR18"
        },
        {
          "label": "Superficial partial thickness burn",
          "next": "depth_t_sup",
          "kind": "trap",
          "trap": "TR18"
        },
        {
          "label": "Deep partial thickness burn",
          "next": "depth_ok",
          "kind": "correct"
        },
        {
          "label": "Not sure, give me a hint",
          "next": "depth_hint",
          "kind": "help"
        }
      ],
      "ask_confidence": true
    },
    "depth_hint": {
      "role": "hint",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Grade the burn by the skin first: its colour, whether it blanches, and how it refills. Then ask whether the pain agrees.",
          "claims": [
            "K25"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "depth_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "depth_t_full": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. Less painful is not the same as painless, and this burn is not insensate.",
          "claims": [
            "K26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Look at the sensation in the stem again. Is the forearm insensate, or only less painful?",
          "claims": [
            "K26"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Show me why",
          "next": "depth_t_full_why",
          "kind": "deeper"
        },
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "depth_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "depth_t_full_why": {
      "role": "explain",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Full thickness tempts because a burn that has lost feeling sounds like the worst burn, and reduced pain seems to tell the same story.",
          "claims": [
            "K26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Deep partial is less painful, mottled and slow to refill. Full thickness is painless, dry, leathery and does not blanch.",
          "claims": [
            "K25",
            "K26"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "depth_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "depth_t_sup": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "That is not right. Superficial partial burns are blistered, moist, pink and very painful, and they blanch.",
          "claims": [
            "K24"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Less pain can look like a shallower step, but this burn is mottled with sluggish refill. Which depth has those signs?",
          "claims": [
            "K25"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Try again",
          "next": "depth_case",
          "kind": "retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "depth_ok": {
      "role": "feedback",
      "objectives": [
        "O5"
      ],
      "say": [
        {
          "text": "Yes. Mottled, less painful and slow to refill fits deep partial thickness. Less pain is a clue to deep injury, not to full thickness.",
          "claims": [
            "K25"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Depth is read by pain, blanching and appearance together. Underestimating depth is the commonest error.",
          "claims": [
            "K26",
            "K28"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Simple erythema is not counted toward the burn area for resuscitation.",
          "claims": [
            "K8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Next",
          "next": "depth_contrast",
          "kind": "continue"
        }
      ]
    },
